From 14373b37019ea6ecfb76c280fe3aac9ecb3fd508 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cedric Robert <cedric.robert@f2i-consulting.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:53:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Sized reads: GetDataArrays without the metadata round trip
 when the caller gives the value count

FesapiHdfProxy::readArrayNdOfValues asks the store for the array metadata
before every read, one round trip out of two on a remote store (about 150 ms
each on a hosted RDDMS), only to decide whether the array fits in one message.
With the sized reads of fesapi (readArrayNdOf...Values taking a
COMMON_NS::Span<T>), the caller gives the value count it expects and the
proxy decides alone.

- FesapiHdfProxy overrides the ten sized reads with readArrayNdOfValuesSized:
  the serialized size of the answer is bounded without asking the store (10
  bytes per value, the worst case of every transport type the store may
  choose, plus the Avro block headers); when that bound fits in one message,
  a single GetDataArrays is sent; otherwise the array is big and the unsized
  path (metadata, then subarrays) is taken as before. A request the store did
  not answer with an array (a protocol exception) falls back to the unsized
  path too. The unsized path takes the capacity as an optional argument.
- GetFullDataArrayHandlers takes the capacity of the buffer: it never writes
  past it, counts the values the store sent (getReceivedValueCount) and
  reports an overflow (hasOverflown), which the proxy turns into a
  std::range_error on the caller's thread. The six transport-type branches
  of the two callbacks become one copy routine, copyAnyArray, shared by the
  full-array and the subarray responses.

Requires the fesapi patches "Sized reads" (Span view and sized virtuals).
Behaviour of the pointer reads is unchanged.
